// A property bag, where the value column has no single type. Demonstrates:
//   • an `any` column, holding numbers and strings in the same position
//   • joining an `any` column against a typed one — a text cell matches a
//     `string` column, and a numeric cell does not, because 1 and "1" are
//     different values (dave's city is the number 1 and joins nothing)
//   • that a cell keeps its JS type: `Born` carries numbers, not "1991"
//
// This is the case `any` exists for. The subject/key/value shape comes from
// somewhere that doesn't have a schema — a front-matter block, a JSON blob, a
// spreadsheet column nobody has characterised — and forcing every value to
// `string` would mean no arithmetic and no joins against typed relations.
//
// Run it:
//     pnpm -F @flow-ts/cli run cli -- examples/props.dl --facts examples
//
// See examples/Prop.csv, examples/City.csv for the seed data.

.in
.decl Prop(subject: string, key: string, value: any)
.input Prop.csv

.decl City(name: string, country: string)
.input City.csv

.out
.decl Born(subject: string, year: any)
.decl Country(subject: string, country: string)
.decl Adult(subject: string)

.rule
// The value column stays whatever it was — these are numbers.
Born(s, v) :- Prop(s, "born", v).

// An `any` cell joins a `string` column when it holds text.
Country(s, c) :- Prop(s, "city", v), City(v, c).

// …and takes part in arithmetic when it holds a number. A row whose `born`
// value were text would fail here by name rather than being coerced.
Adult(s) :- Prop(s, "born", v), 2026 - v >= 18.
